        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Orthostatic intolerance is commonly experienced by astronauts when they return to Earth and can lead to hypotension and syncope (temporary loss of consciousness, fainting, or dizziness), creating a potential hazard for the returning crewmembers. A major driver for the orthostatic intolerance is plasma volume loss (5-19% on landing day). However, while all astronauts have lost plasma volume on landing day, not all astronauts become presyncopal. Those who do and those who do not become presyncopal on landing day are separated by their ability to mount a hyperadrenergic response during landing day stand/tilt tests. A model of hypovolemia that reproduces the plasma volume loss seen on landing day was developed with a regimen of a single dose of IV furosemide, followed by 36 hours of a very low salt diet. This model was tested in a small group of subjects (n=6) that included four astronauts. In the astronauts, this dehydration regimen reproduced the landing day incidence of orthostatic hypotension and presyncope during tilt tests with 100% fidelity. The purpose of this study was to test the utility of this ground-based model by expanding the measurements to include specific hemodynamic and vascular responses, and compare/contrast them with measurements from bed rest studies. This model may be useful for four purposes. First, it may be used to predict which rookie astronauts may become presyncopal on landing day. Second, it may be used to separate the effects of bed rest (or space flight-induced) deconditioning and plasma volume loss on left ventricular mass.  Third, it may be used to determine baseline differences in autonomic response between those who will and those who will not become presyncopal during hypovolemia. Fourth, it may be used to improve a computer model of the cardiovascular consequences of space flight. 
<p>
<i>Specific Aim 1:</i><br> 
To use the hypovolemia model to determine the effect of reduced plasma volume on cardiovascular structure and/or function.
<p>
<i>Specific Aim 2:</i><br> 
To apply the results compiled from the hypovolemia model to a computer model of human physiology and compare these results to those of space flight and bed rest studies that are currently in progress in the laboratory, as well as to data previously gathered. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Twelve subjects (8 males and 4 females) were recruited by the Human Test Subject Facility at NASA Johnson Space Center and all completed a modified Air Force Class III Physical.  Following a familiarization session, the study consisted of three testing days: (1) Normovolemia Test Day, (2) Furosemide Day, and (3) Hypovolemia Test Day.  The Normovolemia test day was the first testing day for half of the subjects while the Furosemide test day was the first testing day for the remaining subjects.  There were at least 7 days between the Normovolemia test day and Furosemide test day.  There were at least 14 days between the Hypovolemia test day and the Normovolemia test day.  
<p>
Plasma volume was measured on the Normovolemia Day and the Hypovolemia Day using a carbon monoxide rebreathing technique.  Briefly, an intravenous catheter was first placed into an antecubital vein.  After 20 minutes of supine rest, a 3 ml baseline blood sample was collected.  Thereafter, subjects breathed 100% oxygen for two minutes using a closed breathing circuit with a two-way non-rebreathing valve (Model 2700, Hans Rudolph, Kansas City, MO).  Sixty ml of carbon monoxide were then added to the system and rebreathed.  After 10 minutes, a second blood sample was collected.  Blood samples were analyzed by the Johnson Space Center Clinical Laboratory for hemoglobin, carboxyhemoglobin, and hematocrit. Total blood volume, red blood cell volume and plasma volume were calculated to confirm adequate plasma volume loss. 
<p>
Five minutes of supine data were collected prior to tilting the subjects to 80 degree head-up tilt using an automatic tilt table.  The subjects remained in this position for 30 minutes, or until symptoms of orthostatic hypotension (systolic blood pressure below 70 mmHg, drop in heart rate > 20 beats per minute<sup>-1</sup> and/or presyncope occurred (lightheadedness, dizziness, or nausea) or per subject request.  Subjects were discouraged from movement, muscle contractions, talking (except to report symptoms), and were encouraged to breathe normally.  Subjects were returned to the supine posture (0 degrees) if they completed the entire 30 minute tilt or were tilted to -10 degrees if the test was terminated due to presyncopal symptoms. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The hypovolemia protocol produced a significant loss of plasma volume. These results are consistent with the decrease in plasma volume reported in the investigators’ previous compression garment study and space flight. Stroke volume was significantly reduced on Hypovolemia Day as compared to Normovolemia Day.  Tilted systolic and diastolic blood pressure, heart rate, stroke volume and cardiac output were significantly different during standing as compared to supine.  Norepinephrine and epinephrine were significantly different at the end of the tilt test as compared to supine.  Norepinephrine, aldosterone, sodium, chloride, creatine, and osmolality were significantly different on Hypovolemia Day as compared to Normovolemia Day.  
<p>
In this study, the investigators’ model of hypovolemia  significantly decreased plasma volume similar to a previous study using furosemide and a low salt diet.  It was also consistent with losses seen following space flight.  While one more subject became presyncopal during hypovolemia, the survival analysis does not show a statistical difference between treatments.  Previous unpublished data, collected in the same laboratory, has shown an increase in presyncope with hypovolemia.  This population seemed to have a greater normovolemic (control) propensity to presyncope, while the presyncope rate during hypovolemia is consistent with the investigators’ other studies, including bed rest.  It is possible that this difference is due to the relatively low subject number, especially when considering the gender breakdown, of this study.  Conversely, it is possible that the population studied here differed in their normovolemic or hypovolemic responses to orthostatic challenges or to the systemic stresses of hypovolemia.
        </div>

